<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computation, Information and Technology &amp; Munich Data Science Institute
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Technical University of Munich
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Temporal point processes (TPPs) are a fundamental tool for modeling event sequences in continuous time, but most existing approaches rely on autoregressive parameterizations that are limited by their sequential sampling.
Recent non-autoregressive, diffusion-style models mitigate these issues by jointly interpolating between noise and data through event insertions and deletions in a discrete Markov chain.
In this work, we generalize this perspective and introduce an Edit Flow process for TPPs that transports noise to data via insert, delete, and substitute edit operations.
By learning the instantaneous edit rates within a continuous-time Markov chain framework, we attain a flexible and efficient model that effectively reduces the total number of necessary edit operations during generation.
Empirical results demonstrate the generative flexibility of our unconditionally trained model in a wide range of unconditional and conditional generation tasks on benchmark TPPs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06050v1/figure1.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:247/218;" width="247" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Edit process transporting <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> by inserting, deleting and substituting events.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">Temporal point processes (TPPs)</span> capture the distribution over sequences of events in time,
where both the continuous arrival-times and number of events are random.
They are widely used in domains such as finance, healthcare, social networks, and transportation, where understanding and forecasting event dynamics and their complex interactions is crucial.
Most (neural) <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> capture the complex interactions between events <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">autoregressively</em>, parameterizing a conditional intensity/density of each event given its history <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daley &amp; Vere-Jones, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
While natural and flexible, this factorization comes with inherent limitations: sampling scales linearly with sequence length, errors can compound in multi-step generation, and conditional generation is restricted to forecasting tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Beyond autoregression.</span>
Recent advances demonstrate that modeling event sequences <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">jointly</em> proposes a sound alternative to overcome these limitations.
Inspired by diffusion, <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AddThin</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PSDiff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverage the thinning and superposition properties of <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> to construct a discrete Markov chain that learns to transform noise sequences <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> into data sequences <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> through <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">insertions</em> and <em id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deletions</em> of events.
These methods highlight the promise of joint sequence modeling for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> by learning stochastic set interpolations and have shown state-of-the-art results, especially in forecasting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In parallel, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduced Edit Flow, a discrete flow-matching framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for variable-length sequences of tokens (e.g., language).
Their approach models discrete flows in sequence space through <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">insertions</em>, <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deletions</em>, and <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">substitutions</em>, formalized as a <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">continuous-time Markov Chain (CTMC)</span>.
To make the learning process tractable, they introduce an expanded auxilliary state space that aligns sequences, simultaneously reducing the complexity of marginalizing over possible transitions and enabling efficient element-wise parameterization in sequence space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we unify these perspectives and propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EdiTPP</span>, an Edit Flow for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> that learns to transport noise sequences <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> to data sequences <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> via atomic <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">edit operations</em> insertions, deletions, and substitutions (see figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We define these operations specifically for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span>, efficiently parameterize their instantaneous rates within a <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span>, propose an auxiliary alignment space for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span>, and show that our unconditionally trained model can be flexibly applied to both unconditional and conditional tasks with adaptive complexity. Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:30pt;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EdiTPP</span>, the first generative framework that models <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> via continuous-time edit operations, unifying stochastic set interpolation methods for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> with Edit Flows for discrete sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a tractable parameterization of insertion, deletion, and substitution rates for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> within the <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> framework, effectively reducing the number of edit operations for generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate empirically that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EdiTPP</span> achieves state-of-the-art results in both unconditional and conditional tasks across diverse real-world and synthetic datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Temporal Point Processes</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daley &amp; Vere-Jones, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daley &amp; Vere-Jones, 2007</a>)</cite> are stochastic processes whose realizations are finite, ordered sets of random events in time.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}=\{t^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}=\{t^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(i)}\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(i)}\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, denote a realization of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> events on a bounded time interval, which can equivalently be represented by the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">counting process</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{t^{(i)}\leq t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{t^{(i)}\leq t\}</annotation></semantics></math> counting the number of events up to time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> is uniquely characterized by its <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional intensity function</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasmussen, 2018</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda^{*}(t)=\lim\limits_{\Delta t\downarrow 0}\frac{\mathbb{E}[N(t+\Delta t)-N(t)\mid\mathcal{H}_{t}]}{\Delta t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda^{*}(t)=\lim\limits_{\Delta t\downarrow 0}\frac{\mathbb{E}[N(t+\Delta t)-N(t)\mid\mathcal{H}_{t}]}{\Delta t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}=\{t^{(i)}:t^{(i)}&lt;t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}=\{t^{(i)}:t^{(i)}&lt;t\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the history up to time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{*}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{*}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the instantaneous rate of events given the past.
Two important properties of <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> are superposition and thinning.
Superposition, i.e., <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inserting</span> one sequence into another, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}={\bm{t}}_{1}\cup{\bm{t}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}={\bm{t}}_{1}\cup{\bm{t}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are realizations from <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> with intensities <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, results in a sample from a <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> with intensity <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Independent thinning, i.e., randomly <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deleting</span> any event of a sequence from a <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> with intensity <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, results in an event sequence from a <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> with intensity <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-p)\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-p)\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The likelihood of observing an event sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> given the conditional intensity/density is:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\bm{t}})=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(t^{(i)}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t^{(i)}})\right)\left(1-F(T\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})\right)=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}\lambda^{*}(t^{(i)})\right)\text{ exp }\left(-\int_{0}^{T}\lambda^{*}(s)\mathrm{d}s\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext> exp </mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{t}})=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(t^{(i)}\mid\mathcal{H}_{t^{(i)}})\right)\left(1-F(T\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})\right)=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}\lambda^{*}(t^{(i)})\right)\text{ exp }\left(-\int_{0}^{T}\lambda^{*}(s)\mathrm{d}s\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the CDF of the conditional event density <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t\mid\mathcal{H}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
While this autoregressive formulation of <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> provides a natural framework for modeling event dependencies, it also poses challenges.
Parameterizing the conditional intensity or density is generally nontrivial, and the inherently sequential factorization can lead to inefficient sampling, error accumulation, and limits conditional tasks to forecasting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Modeling TPPs by set interpolation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of explicitly modeling the intensity function, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al. (2025)</a></cite> leverage the thinning and superposition properties of <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> to derive diffusion-like generative models that interpolate between data event sequences <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{target}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> and noise <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> by inserting and deleting elements.
<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AddThin</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>)</cite> defines the noising Markov chain recursively over a fixed number of steps with size <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}(t)=\underbrace{\alpha_{s}\lambda_{s-\Delta}(t)}_{\text{(i) Thin}}+\underbrace{(1-\alpha_{s})\lambda_{\mathrm{0}}(t)}_{\text{(ii) Add}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(i) Thin</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>(ii) Add</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}(t)=\underbrace{\alpha_{s}\lambda_{s-\Delta}(t)}_{\text{(i) Thin}}+\underbrace{(1-\alpha_{s})\lambda_{\mathrm{0}}(t)}_{\text{(ii) Add}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown target intensity of the <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{s}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{s}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, this noising process increasingly deletes events from the data sequence, while inserting events from a noise <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{0}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{0}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PSDiff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> further separates the adding and thinning to yield a Markov chain for the forward process, that stochastically interpolates between <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})=\prod_{t\in{\bm{t}}}\begin{cases}\bar{\alpha}_{s}&amp;\text{if }t\in{\bm{t}}_{1}\\
1-\bar{\alpha}_{s}&amp;\text{if }t\in{\bm{t}}_{0}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒕</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})=\prod_{t\in{\bm{t}}}\begin{cases}\bar{\alpha}_{s}&amp;\text{if }t\in{\bm{t}}_{1}\\
1-\bar{\alpha}_{s}&amp;\text{if }t\in{\bm{t}}_{0}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">or equivalently <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}(t)=\bar{\alpha}_{s}\lambda_{1}(t)+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{s})\lambda_{0}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}(t)=\bar{\alpha}_{s}\lambda_{1}(t)+(1-\bar{\alpha}_{s})\lambda_{0}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> being the product of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s.
<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.2 Modeling TPPs by set interpolation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> defines an element-wise conditional path by independent insert and delete operations on <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span>, assuming <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\cap{\bm{t}}_{1}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\cap{\bm{t}}_{1}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Flow matching with edit operations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce Edit Flows, a non-autoregressive generative framework for variable-length token sequences with a fixed, discrete vocabulary (e.g., language).
They propose a discrete flow that transports a noisy sequence <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> to a data sequence <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{data}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{data}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> via elementary <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edit operations</span>: insertions, deletions, and substitutions.
This is formalized via the discrete flow matching framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> in an augmented space, yielding a <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(X_{s+h}={\bm{x}}\mid X_{s}={\bm{x}}_{s})=\delta_{{\bm{x}}_{s}}({\bm{x}})+hu_{s}^{\theta}({\bm{x}}\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})+o(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(X_{s+h}={\bm{x}}\mid X_{s}={\bm{x}}_{s})=\delta_{{\bm{x}}_{s}}({\bm{x}})+hu_{s}^{\theta}({\bm{x}}\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})+o(h)</annotation></semantics></math> with transition rates <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> governed by the edit operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Directly defining a conditional rate <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}({\bm{x}}|{\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{x}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}({\bm{x}}|{\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> to match <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to, as in discrete flow matching, is very hard or even intractable, since all possible edits producing <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> must be considered.
Thus, to train this <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span>, they rely on two major insights.
First, a <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> in a data space <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> can be learned by introducing an augmented space <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> where the true dynamics are known.
Second, designing the auxiliary space <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> to follow the element wise mixture probability path <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}({\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})=\prod_{n}\big[(1-\kappa_{s})\delta_{z_{0}^{(i)}}(z^{(i)})+\kappa_{s}\delta_{z_{1}^{(i)}}(z^{(i)})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}({\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})=\prod_{n}\big[(1-\kappa_{s})\delta_{z_{0}^{(i)}}(z^{(i)})+\kappa_{s}\delta_{z_{1}^{(i)}}(z^{(i)})\big]</annotation></semantics></math> with kappa schedule <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{s}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{s}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite> enables training the <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> directly in the data space <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of variable-length sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Edit operations are encoded by mapping <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{0},{\bm{x}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{0},{\bm{x}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> into aligned sequences <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, where pairs <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z_{0}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z_{0}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to insertions <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, deletions <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, or substitutions <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Crucially, since the discrete flow matching dynamics in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> are known, they can be transferred back to <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{s}({\bm{x}},{\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})=p_{s}({\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\delta_{\rmblanks({\bm{z}})}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}({\bm{x}},{\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})=p_{s}({\bm{z}}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\delta_{\rmblanks({\bm{z}})}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the marginal rates <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> by marginalizing over <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the Bregman divergence</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\E_{\begin{subarray}{c}({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\sim\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\\
s,p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s},{\bm{x}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\end{subarray}}\bigg[\sum_{{\bm{x}}\neq{\bm{x}}_{s}}u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}({\bm{x}}\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})-\sum_{z_{s}^{(i)}\neq z_{1}^{(i)}}\frac{\dot{\kappa}_{s}}{1-\kappa_{s}}\log u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}\big({\bm{x}}({\bm{z}}_{s},i,z_{1}^{(i)})\mid{\bm{x}}_{s}\big)\bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\E_{\begin{subarray}{c}({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\sim\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\\
s,p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s},{\bm{x}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\end{subarray}}\bigg[\sum_{{\bm{x}}\neq{\bm{x}}_{s}}u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}({\bm{x}}\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})-\sum_{z_{s}^{(i)}\neq z_{1}^{(i)}}\frac{\dot{\kappa}_{s}}{1-\kappa_{s}}\log u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}\big({\bm{x}}({\bm{z}}_{s},i,z_{1}^{(i)})\mid{\bm{x}}_{s}\big)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}({\bm{z}}_{s},i,z_{1}^{(i)})=\rmblanks((z_{s}^{(1)},\dots,z_{s}^{(i-1)},z_{1}^{(i)},z_{s}^{(i+1)},\dots,z_{s}^{(n)}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}({\bm{z}}_{s},i,z_{1}^{(i)})=\rmblanks((z_{s}^{(1)},\dots,z_{s}^{(i-1)},z_{1}^{(i)},z_{s}^{(i+1)},\dots,z_{s}^{(n)}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EdiTPP</span>, an Edit Flow process for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span> that directly learns the joint distribution of event times.
Our process leverages the three elementary edit operations <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">insert</em>, <em id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">substitute</em>, and <em id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">delete</em> to define a <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> that continuously interpolates between two event sequences <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{data}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim q_{\text{data}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the support of the <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span>.
We define the state space as <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}\;=\;\bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty}\left\{(0,t^{(1)},\dots,t^{(n)},T)\in\mathcal{T}^{n}:0&lt;t^{(1)}&lt;\cdots&lt;t^{(n)}&lt;T\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}\;=\;\bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty}\left\{(0,t^{(1)},\dots,t^{(n)},T)\in\mathcal{T}^{n}:0&lt;t^{(1)}&lt;\cdots&lt;t^{(n)}&lt;T\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoting the set of all possible <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">padded</em> <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPP</span> sequences with finitely many events.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Edit operations</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06050v1/figure2.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/147;" width="466" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Our discrete edit operations transform continuous event sequences through insertions, substitutions and deletion.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model navigates the state space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> through a set of atomic edit operations.
While Edit Flow was originally defined for discrete state spaces, we can generalize the method to continuous state spaces provided that the set of edit operations remains discrete.
We achieve this by defining a finite set of edit operations on our continuous state space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> that nonetheless allow us to transition from any sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> to any other <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> through repeated application.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we design our operations to be mutually exclusive: if two sequences differ by exactly one edit, the responsible operation is uniquely determined.
This simplifies the parameterization of the model and computation of the Bregman divergence in <a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.3 Flow matching with edit operations ‣ 2 Background ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insertion</span>: To discretize the event insertion, we quantize the space between any two adjacent events <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(i+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(i+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\text{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\text{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math> evenly-spaced bins.
Then, we define the insertion operation relative to the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th event as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ins({\bm{t}},i,j)=\Big(t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i)},t^{(i)}+\frac{j-1+\alpha}{b_{\text{ins}}}(t^{(i+1)}-t^{(i)}),t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)}\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ins</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ins({\bm{t}},i,j)=\Big(t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i)},t^{(i)}+\frac{j-1+\alpha}{b_{\text{ins}}}(t^{(i+1)}-t^{(i)}),t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{0,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{0,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dequantization factor inspired by uniform dequantization in likelihood-based generative models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Theis et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
The boundary elements <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(0)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(0)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(n+1)}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(n+1)}=T</annotation></semantics></math> ensure that insertions are possible across the entire support <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the bins between different <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> are non-overlapping, insertions are mutually exclusive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Substitution</span>: We implement event substitutions by discretizing the continuous space around each event into <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> bins.
In this case, the bins are free to overlap, since a substitution is always uniquely determined by the substituted event.
We choose a maximum movement distance <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and define</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sub({\bm{t}},i,j)=\operatorname{sort}\Big(\{t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i-1)},t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)}\}\cup\Big\{\tilde{t}^{(i)}\Big\}\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sort</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sub({\bm{t}},i,j)=\operatorname{sort}\Big(\{t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i-1)},t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)}\}\cup\Big\{\tilde{t}^{(i)}\Big\}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}^{(i)}=\big[t^{(i)}-\delta+\frac{j-1+\alpha}{b_{\text{sub}}}2\delta\big]_{0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}^{(i)}=\big[t^{(i)}-\delta+\frac{j-1+\alpha}{b_{\text{sub}}}2\delta\big]_{0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the updated event restricted to the support <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and, again, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a uniform dequantization factor within the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th bin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deletion</span>: Finally, we define removing event <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> straightforwardly as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\del({\bm{t}},i)=(t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i-1)},t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>del</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\del({\bm{t}},i)=(t^{(0)},\dots,t^{(i-1)},t^{(i+1)},\dots,t^{(n+1)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In combination, these operations facilitate any possible edit of an event sequence through insertions and deletions with substitutions as a shortcut for local delete-insert pairs.
Note that we neither allow inserting after the last boundary event nor substituting or deleting the first or last boundary events, thus guaranteeing operations to stay in the state space <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We illustrate the edit operations in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Edit operations ‣ 3 Method ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parameterization</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generating a new event sequence in the Edit Flow framework then means to emit a continuous stream of edit operations by integrating a rate model <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The emitted operations transform a noise sequence <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into a data sample <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by transitioning through a series of intermediate states <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a current state <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we parameterize the transition rates as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ins</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}({\bm{t}}_{s})\,Q_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}(j\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}({\bm{t}}_{s})\,Q_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}(j\mid{\bm{t}}_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}}_{s})\,Q_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}(j\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}}_{s})\,Q_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}(j\mid{\bm{t}}_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>del</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}}_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>del</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}}_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}},\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}},\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>del</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}},\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}},\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the total rate of each of the three basic operations at each event <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The distributions <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">categorical</em> distributions over the discretization bins <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
They distribute the total insertion and substitution rates between the specific options.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Auxiliary alignment space</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training our rate model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by directly matching a marginalized conditional rate <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> generating a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}({\bm{t}}\mid{\bm{t}}_{1},{\bm{t}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, as is common in discrete flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
is challenging or even intractable for Edit Flows, since it would require accounting for all possible edits that could produce <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:30%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06050v1/figure3.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:165/139;" width="165" height="139"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Illustration of the alignment space for <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we introduce an auxiliary alignment space for <span title="temporal point process" class="ltx_glossaryref">TPPs</span>, where every possible edit operation is uniquely defined in the element wise mixture path <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}\sim p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}\sim p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, making the learning problem tractable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In language modeling, any token can appear in any position, so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al. (2025)</a></cite> achieve strong results even when training with a simple alignment that juxtaposes two sequences after shifting one of them by a constant number of places.
In our case, for the alignments to correspond to possible edit operations, two events can only be matched, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z_{0}^{(i)}-z_{1}^{(i)}|&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z_{0}^{(i)}-z_{1}^{(i)}|&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math> since otherwise the resulting <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sub" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sub</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sub</annotation></semantics></math> operation would be invalid.
Furthermore, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>s have to correspond to sequences in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rmblanks({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rmblanks({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> has to be increasing, and in particular any mixing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be valid, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}\sim p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\Rightarrow\rmblanks({\bm{z}}_{s})\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}\sim p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\Rightarrow\rmblanks({\bm{z}}_{s})\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We find the minimum-cost alignment between the non-boundary events of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the Needleman-Wunsch algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Needleman &amp; Wunsch, 1970</a>)</cite>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alignseqs({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})=\operatorname{wrap-boundaries}\!\bigg(\!\operatorname{Needleman-Wunsch}\!\Big({\bm{t}}_{0}^{(1:n)},{\bm{t}}_{1}^{(1:m)},c_{\text{ins}},c_{\text{sub}},c_{\text{del}}\Big)\!\bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>align</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>wrap</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>boundaries</mi></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Needleman</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Wunsch</mi></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>del</mtext></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alignseqs({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})=\operatorname{wrap-boundaries}\!\bigg(\!\operatorname{Needleman-Wunsch}\!\Big({\bm{t}}_{0}^{(1:n)},{\bm{t}}_{1}^{(1:m)},c_{\text{ins}},c_{\text{sub}},c_{\text{del}}\Big)\!\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and the cost functions</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}c_{\text{sub}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}|t_{0}^{(i)}-t_{1}^{(j)}|&amp;\text{if }|t_{0}^{(i)}-t_{1}^{(j)}|&lt;\delta\text{ and }t_{0}^{(i-1)}&lt;t_{1}^{(j)}&lt;t_{0}^{(i+1)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\\
c_{\text{ins}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}\frac{\delta}{2}&amp;\text{if }t_{0}^{(i)}&lt;t_{1}^{(j)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\qquad c_{\text{del}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}\frac{\delta}{2}&amp;\text{if }t_{0}^{(i)}&gt;t_{1}^{(j)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:4.000em;" width="4.000em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>del</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}c_{\text{sub}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}|t_{0}^{(i)}-t_{1}^{(j)}|&amp;\text{if }|t_{0}^{(i)}-t_{1}^{(j)}|&lt;\delta\text{ and }t_{0}^{(i-1)}&lt;t_{1}^{(j)}&lt;t_{0}^{(i+1)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\\
c_{\text{ins}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}\frac{\delta}{2}&amp;\text{if }t_{0}^{(i)}&lt;t_{1}^{(j)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\qquad c_{\text{del}}(i,j)=\begin{cases}\frac{\delta}{2}&amp;\text{if }t_{0}^{(i)}&gt;t_{1}^{(j)}\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{wrap-boundaries}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>wrap</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>boundaries</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{wrap-boundaries}</annotation></semantics></math> wraps the sequences with aligned boundary events <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
The algorithm builds up the aligned sequences pair by pair.
The operations corresponds to adding different pairs to the end of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., insertion <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\epsilon,t_{1}^{(j)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\epsilon,t_{1}^{(j)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, deletion <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(t_{0}^{(i)},\epsilon\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(t_{0}^{(i)},\epsilon\big)</annotation></semantics></math> and substitution <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(t_{0}^{(i)},t_{1}^{(j)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(t_{0}^{(i)},t_{1}^{(j)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2 Auxiliary alignment space ‣ 3 Method ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is trivial to see from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> that the aligned sequences will never encode a <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sub" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sub</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sub</annotation></semantics></math> operation for two events that are further than <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> apart.
The costs for insertions and deletions and the additional condition on <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> ensure that the aligned sequences are jointly sorted, i.e., for any <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\big(z_{0}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)&lt;\min\big(z_{0}^{(j)},z_{1}^{(j)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\big(z_{0}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)&lt;\min\big(z_{0}^{(j)},z_{1}^{(j)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> ignore <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
This means that any interpolated <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> will be sorted.
The validity of encoded <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ins" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ins</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ins</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\del" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>del</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\del</annotation></semantics></math> operations follows immediately.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train our model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing the Bregman divergence in <a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.3 Flow matching with edit operations ‣ 2 Background ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
This amounts to sampling from a coupling <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> in the aligned auxiliary space and then matching the ground-truth conditional event rates.
Note that the coupling <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is implicitly defined by its sampling procedure: sample <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim\pi({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1}\sim\pi({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> from a coupling of the noise and data distribution, e.g., the independent coupling <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})=p({\bm{t}}_{0})\,q({\bm{t}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})=p({\bm{t}}_{0})\,q({\bm{t}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and then align the sequences <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1}=\alignseqs({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>align</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1}=\alignseqs({\bm{t}}_{0},{\bm{t}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For our choice of operations, the divergence is</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\E_{\begin{subarray}{c}({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\sim\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\\
s,p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s},{\bm{t}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\end{subarray}}\bigg[\sum_{\omega\in\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})}u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\omega\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})-\sum_{z_{s}^{(i)}\neq z_{1}^{(i)}}\frac{\dot{\kappa}_{s}}{1-\kappa_{s}}\log u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}\Big(\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s}\Big)\bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\E_{\begin{subarray}{c}({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\sim\pi({\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\\
s,p_{s}({\bm{z}}_{s},{\bm{t}}_{s}\mid{\bm{z}}_{0},{\bm{z}}_{1})\end{subarray}}\bigg[\sum_{\omega\in\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})}u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\omega\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})-\sum_{z_{s}^{(i)}\neq z_{1}^{(i)}}\frac{\dot{\kappa}_{s}}{1-\kappa_{s}}\log u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}\Big(\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)\mid{\bm{t}}_{s}\Big)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all edit operations applicable to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> is the edit operation encoded in the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th position of the aligned sequences <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To make it precise, we have</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})=\bigcup\begin{cases}\{\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid i\in\{0\}\,\cup\,[n],j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]\}\\
\{\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid i\in[n],j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]\}\\
\{\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i)\mid i\in[n]\}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ins</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo rspace="0.392em">∪</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>sub</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>del</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega({\bm{t}}_{s})=\bigcup\begin{cases}\{\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid i\in\{0\}\,\cup\,[n],j\in[b_{\text{ins}}]\}\\
\{\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i,j)\mid i\in[n],j\in[b_{\text{sub}}]\}\\
\{\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i)\mid i\in[n]\}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)=\begin{cases}\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime},j^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}=\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon,\\
\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime},j^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon,\\
\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}=\epsilon.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ins</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>sub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>del</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\big(z_{s}^{(i)},z_{1}^{(i)}\big)=\begin{cases}\ins({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime},j^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}=\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon,\\
\sub({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime},j^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon,\\
\del({\bm{t}}_{s},i^{\prime})&amp;\text{if }z_{s}^{(i)}\neq\epsilon\text{ and }z_{1}^{(i)}=\epsilon.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index such that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rmblanks({\bm{z}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rmblanks({\bm{z}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}^{(i^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}^{(i^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> with the convention that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to the same <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the last element of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> before <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> that is not <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index of the insertion or substitution bin relative to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{s}^{(i^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}^{(i^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> falls into.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Sampling</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_figure ltx_algorithm ltx_align_floatright ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:55%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Conditional Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{condition }{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{1}),\text{ noise }{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}},h=1/n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>condition </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> noise </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{condition }{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{1}),\text{ noise }{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}},h=1/n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{z}}_{0}^{c},{\bm{z}}_{1}^{c})\leftarrow\alignseqs(C({\bm{t}}_{0}),{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>align</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{z}}_{0}^{c},{\bm{z}}_{1}^{c})\leftarrow\alignseqs(C({\bm{t}}_{0}),{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="alg1.4.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="alg1.4.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="alg1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euler update</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Sample edits <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{s}\sim h\,u_{s}^{\theta}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>h</mi><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{s}\sim h\,u_{s}^{\theta}(\cdot\mid{\bm{t}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s+h}\leftarrow\text{apply }\omega_{s}\text{ to }{\bm{t}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>apply </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s+h}\leftarrow\text{apply }\omega_{s}\text{ to }{\bm{t}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="alg1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recondition</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{s+h}^{c}\sim p_{s+h}(\cdot\mid{\bm{z}}_{0}^{c},{\bm{z}}_{1}^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{s+h}^{c}\sim p_{s+h}(\cdot\mid{\bm{z}}_{0}^{c},{\bm{z}}_{1}^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s+h}^{c}\leftarrow\rmblanks(\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{s+h}^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>f_{rm-blanks}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s+h}^{c}\leftarrow\rmblanks(\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{s+h}^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="alg1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Merge</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s+h}\leftarrow C^{\prime}({\bm{t}}_{s+h})\cup{\bm{t}}_{s+h}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s+h}\leftarrow C^{\prime}({\bm{t}}_{s+h})\cup{\bm{t}}_{s+h}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leftarrow s+h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leftarrow s+h</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline">end while
</div>
<div id="alg1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return:</span> forecast trajectory <math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}({\bm{t}}_{s=1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}({\bm{t}}_{s=1})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sampling from our model is done by forward simulation of the <span title="continuous-time Markov Chain" class="ltx_glossaryref">CTMC</span> from noise <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}\sim p_{\text{noise}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and leverage their Euler approximation, since exact simulation is intractable.
Even though the rates are parameterized per element, sampling multiple edits within a time horizon can be done in parallel.
At each step of length <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, insertions at position <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> occur with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\,\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\,\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> and deletions or substitutions occur with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}})+\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>del</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}})+\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since they are mutually exclusive the probability of substitution vs deletion is <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}})/(\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}})+\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>del</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}})/(\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}({\bm{t}})+\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}({\bm{t}}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Lastly, the inserted or substituted events are drawn from the respective distributions <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to update <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a short summary of the unconditional sampling step refer to the Euler update step depicted in algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.4 Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conditional sampling.</span> We can extend the unconditional model to conditional generation given a binary mask on time <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., for forecasting, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(t)=t\leq t_{\text{history}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>history</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(t)=t\leq t_{\text{history}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For a sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the conditioned part <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C({\bm{t}})=\{t\in{\bm{t}}:c(t)=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒕</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C({\bm{t}})=\{t\in{\bm{t}}:c(t)=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and its complement <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}({\bm{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}({\bm{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then as depicted in algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.4 Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for conditional sampling, we can simply enforce the conditional subsequence to follow a noisy interpolation between <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{0}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{0}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{t}}_{1}^{c}=C({\bm{t}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the complement evolves freely in the sampling process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Model Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our rate model <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{{\bm{\theta}}}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>, we adapt the Llama architecture, a transformer widely applied for variable-length sequences in language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We employ <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FlexAttention</span> in the Llama attention blocks, which supports variable-length sequences natively without padding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
As a first step, we convert the scalar event sequence <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> into a sequence of token embeddings by applying <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MLP}(\operatorname{SinEmb}(x_{s}^{(i)}/T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MLP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SinEmb</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MLP}(\operatorname{SinEmb}(x_{s}^{(i)}/T))</annotation></semantics></math> to each to each event, where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MLP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MLP</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MLP}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a small <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">multi-layer perceptron (MLP)</span> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{SinEmb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>SinEmb</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{SinEmb}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sinusoidal embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
We convert <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\bm{x}}_{s}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\bm{x}}_{s}|</annotation></semantics></math> into two additional tokens in an equivalent way with separate <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">MLPs</span> and prepend them to the embedding sequence, which we then feed to the Llama.
Lastly, we apply one more <span title="multi-layer perceptron" class="ltx_glossaryref">MLP</span> to map the output embedding <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> of each event to transition rates.
In particular, we parameterize</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{ins}}^{(i)})),\quad\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{sub}}^{(i)})),\quad\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{del}}^{(i)}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mtext>ins</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mtext>sub</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>del</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>M</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mtext>del</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{ins}}^{(i)})),\quad\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{sub}}^{(i)})),\quad\lambda_{s,i}^{\text{del}}=\exp(\lambda_{\text{M}}\tanh({\bm{h}}_{\text{del}}^{(i)})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{Q}}_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{h}}_{{\bm{Q}},\text{ins}}^{(i)}),\quad{\bm{Q}}_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{h}}_{{\bm{Q}},\text{sub}}^{(i)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>ins</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ins</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>sub</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{Q}}_{s,i}^{\text{ins}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{h}}_{{\bm{Q}},\text{ins}}^{(i)}),\quad{\bm{Q}}_{s,i}^{\text{sub}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{h}}_{{\bm{Q}},\text{sub}}^{(i)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We list the values of all relevant hyperparameters in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Model Parameters ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our model on seven real-world and six synthetic benchmark datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Omi et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In our experiments, we compare against <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">IFTPP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, an autoregressive baseline which consistently shows state-of-the-art performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bosser &amp; Taieb, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kerrigan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
We further compare to <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PSDiff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AddThin</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, given their strong results in both conditional and unconditional settings and their methodological similarity to our approach.
All models are trained with five seeds and we select the best checkpoint based on <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-over-<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{IET}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>IET</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{IET}}</annotation></semantics></math> against a validation set.
<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EdiTPP</span>, <span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AddThin</span>, and <span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PSDiff</span> are trained unconditionally but can be conditioned at inference time.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          To stay comparable, we employ the conditioning algorithm from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüdke et al. (2025)</a></cite> for <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AddThin</span>.</span></span></span>
We list the full results in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Detailed Results ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For forecasts, we compare predicted and target sequences by three metrics:
<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{Xiao}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>Xiao</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{Xiao}}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2017)</a></cite>, the <span title="mean relative error" class="ltx_glossaryref">mean relative error (MRE)</span> of the event counts and <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{IET}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>IET</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{IET}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which compares inter-event times to quantify the relation between events such as burstiness.
In unconditional generation, we compare our generated sequences to the test set in terms of <span title="maximum mean discrepancy" class="ltx_glossaryref">maximum mean discrepancy (MMD)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shchur et al., 2020b</a>)</cite> and their Wasserstein-1 distance with respect to their counts (<math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) and inter-event times (<math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{IET}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>IET</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{IET}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
See <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Metrics ‣ Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Unconditional generation</h3>

